# SwiftFormat configuration for this repository.
#
# RULES URL: https://github.com/nicklockwood/SwiftFormat/blob/main/Rules.md
# OPTIONS:   swiftformat --options
# RULE INFO: swiftformat --ruleinfo <name>
#
# SwiftFormat FIXES, SwiftLint REPORTS (.swiftlint.yml). A rule is enabled here when it repairs something the
# linter would report, and disabled when it would rewrite code into a lint violation, against a style this code
# keeps on purpose, or into a different public API.
#
# Every option and every --disable below was measured against this tree (SwiftFormat 0.64.4, 2026-10-30): the
# number in parentheses is how many sites the default would have rewritten.
#
# Run it with Tools/SwiftFormat/format.sh, before Tools/SwiftLint/lint.sh.


# --- Options ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --swift-version gates the rewrites a rule may emit: it is the oldest compiler that builds this package
# (swift-tools-version 6.4). At 6.4 `redundantParens` writes an optional existential as `any P?` instead of
# `(any P)?`, the same type in either language mode.
--swift-version 6.4
--language-mode 6

# Multi-line collection literals end with a comma (74 did, 13 did not); argument and parameter lists do not
# (4 did, all in the manifests). The default, `always`, adds one to every wrapped call. (180)
--trailing-commas collections-only

# Format bytes are written in lowercase hex, `0xcc`. (476)
--hex-literal-case lowercase

# The default grouping thresholds strip the underscores from literals such as `10_000`. (25)
--decimal-grouping ignore
--hex-grouping ignore
--binary-grouping ignore
--octal-grouping ignore

# Access modifiers sit on the declarations, not on the extension. (24, in 3 files)
--extension-acl on-declarations

# Test functions are named in camelCase, not with raw identifiers. (253)
--test-case-name-format preserve

# The code writes `case .foo(let bar)`, not `case let .foo(bar)`. (hoist: 120; inline: 10)
#
# KNOWN DEFECT: when the patterns of one `catch` or `case` sit on separate lines, the rewrite leaves the
# continuation lines at column 1, which SwiftLint's `indentation_width` reports. Re-indent them by hand once;
# the next pass leaves them alone.
--pattern-let inline

# `0..<count`, with no spaces. (96)
--ranges no-space

# public import -> package import -> import, alphabetical within each group; each blank-line-separated block
# (the tests' `@testable import`) is sorted on its own. (hoist: 43)
--import-grouping access-control,alpha
--hoist-imports preserve

# A wrapped call closes on a line of its own, as SwiftLint's multiline_arguments_brackets asks. Most did; the
# 126 that closed on their last argument's line were moved when this file was added.
--closing-paren balanced

# Any wrap value other than `preserve` can collapse a bracket back onto its content line, which
# multiline_literal_brackets, multiline_parameters_brackets, multiline_arguments_brackets and
# vertical_parameter_alignment_on_call report. These are the current defaults, pinned so a future default
# change cannot start a fight with the linter.
--wrap-arguments preserve
--wrap-collections preserve
--wrap-conditions preserve
--wrap-type-aliases preserve
--wrap-return-type preserve
--wrap-effects preserve


# --- Excludes -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Mirrors .gitignore. Hidden directories (.build, .swiftpm) are skipped anyway.

--exclude DerivedData,Packages


# --- Rules enabled beyond the defaults ----------------------------------------------------------

# Each clears the SwiftLint rule named after it.
--enable isEmpty                  # empty_count, which reports at ERROR severity
--enable preferContains           # contains_over_filter_is_empty, contains_over_first_not_nil
--enable preferFirstWhere         # first_where
--enable preferCountWhere         # contains_over_filter_count
--enable preferMinOverSorted      # sorted_first_last, for .first only
--enable preferFlatMap            # flatmap_over_map_reduce, reduce_into

# No SwiftLint pairing: each repairs a construct the first time somebody writes it, at no risk to working code.
--enable emptyExtensions          # an extension that conforms to something is left alone
--enable preferSwiftStringAPI     # `replacingOccurrences(of:with:)` -> `replacing(_:with:)`
--enable singlePropertyPerLine    # `let a, b: Int` -> one declaration per line
--enable commonTypos              # comments and private names only
--enable preferSwiftTesting       # converted the macro tests too: assertMacroExpansion still records its
                                  # failures as Swift Testing issues (checked by breaking an expansion)


# --- Disabled rules -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Turns `public struct MessagePackSerializer`, which only hosts static members, into an enum: a source and ABI
# break for every 0.1.0 user. SwiftLint's convenience_type stays off for the same reason. (1)
--disable enumNamespaces

# Moves the opening brace of a multi-line statement onto its own line, which SwiftLint's `opening_brace`
# reports; on a multi-line `guard` it leaves `else` alone at the end of the condition. (2)
--disable wrapMultilineStatementBraces

# Indentation is Xcode's (Ctrl+I) and is checked by SwiftLint's `indentation_width`. SwiftFormat indents a
# wrapped operand one level deeper than Xcode does, so the two would ping-pong. (492)
--disable indent

# With `indent` off, a rule that changes nesting depth leaves the body at its old indentation. (0)
--disable redundantClosure

# A raw value is what gets encoded: once it is implicit, renaming the case silently changes it. SwiftLint's
# redundant_string_enum_value is disabled for the same reason. (0)
--disable redundantRawValues

# The tests spell `Optional<Int>.none`, and a `Swift.Optional<Int>` field, on purpose: the macro must recognise
# the long form. SwiftLint's syntactic_sugar is disabled to match. (5)
--disable typeSugar

# Writes `public nonisolated`, while SwiftLint's `modifier_order` wants `nonisolated public`. (0)
--disable modifierOrder

# Removes the blank line before the tests' `@testable import` block. (13)
--disable blankLinesBetweenImports

# Promotes `//` comments above declarations into `///` documentation; those comments explain how the code
# works, they are not API documentation. (51)
--disable docComments

# Collapses the two spaces this code writes before a trailing comment, `writeByte(0xd6)  // fixext 4`. (97)
--disable consecutiveSpaces

# Deliberate style this code writes consistently.
--disable redundantSelf            # (26)  `self.` in initializers
--disable wrapPropertyBodies       # (69)  one-line computed properties
--disable conditionalAssignment    # (27)  a declaration followed by the `switch` that assigns it
--disable unusedArguments          # (22)  protocol witnesses, where the name documents the argument
--disable wrapIfStatementBodies    # (68)  one-line early exits and the conformance-cache lookups
--disable wrapIfExpressionBodies   # (32)  the same for `if case … { true } else { false }`
--disable preferForLoop            # (0)   converts only multi-line forEach, leaving the code more mixed
--disable hoistTry                 # (174) moves `try` onto calls that do not throw
--disable opaqueGenericParameters  # (13)  rewrites named generics to `some`, public API among them
--disable wrapFunctionBodies       # (139) the one-line Codable container witnesses
--disable repeatedGuard            # (2)   each precondition keeps its own guard


# --- Audited and rejected -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Off by default, so no --disable line is needed; this is the record of why.
#
#   preferLazyMap              `map { … }.joined()` -> `lazy.map { … }.joined()` changes how the code
#                              evaluates; in the library that is a measurement, not a format. (2)
#   noExplicitOwnership        Strips `borrowing`/`consuming`, which this code places by measurement. (9)
#   redundantSendable          Inference does not cross a module boundary, so stripping an explicit
#                              `Sendable` from a public type changes its API. (0)
#   acronyms, preferFinalClasses, redundantOverload, unusedPrivateDeclarations
#                              Rename, seal, merge or delete declarations without seeing their clients. (0)
#   forWhere                   Moves `isKnownUniquelyReferenced(&…)` into a `where` clause; SwiftLint's
#                              for_where is configured to accept a loop used as a filter. (1 loop)
#   Too much churn to be this code's style: organizeDeclarations (1264), markTypes (221), wrapCaseBodies (119),
#   wrapGuardStatementBodies (111), blankLinesAfterGuardStatements (104), blankLineAfterSwitchCase (84),
#   propertyTypes (76), wrapSwitchCases (25), preferExplicitFalse (21), wrapEnumCases (15),
#   sortSwitchCases (8), ifExpressions (2).
